<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quintus Servilius Caepio (consul 106 BCE)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quintus Servilius Caepio was a Roman general and consul in 106 BCE. He captured Tolosa (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>) from the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volcae">Volcae</a> Tectosages and seized its temple treasure, the 'gold of Tolosa', which disappeared on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 105 BCE his refusal to cooperate with the consul Gnaeus Mallius Maximus led to the disaster of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>, and he was later condemned and went into exile.</p></div>
